WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:02.760
SPEAKER 1: Mr. James Bowman
is the film and culture

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.890
critic for The
American Spectator

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:08.280
and the media columnist
for The New Criterion.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.710
He also writes a weekly
column on the media

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.510
for The Epoch Times.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:15.360
A graduate of Lebanon Valley
College in Pennsylvania

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.090
and Cambridge University,
he is a resident scholar

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:22.220
at the Ethics and Public Policy
Center in Washington, DC.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:24.410
He previously taught
English and general studies

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:27.483
in London and Portsmouth,
England for nine years.

00:00:27.483 --> 00:00:29.900
He returned to the United
States as American correspondent

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.910
for The Spectator
and later served

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:35.610
as the American editor of The
Times Literary Supplement.

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:39.720
He is the author of Honor:
A History and Media Madness:

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.120
the Corruption of our
Political Culture.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:43.440
Please welcome James Bowman.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.870
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:51.280
JAMES BOWMAN: Thank you.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.450
There's no knowing for sure--

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:15.350
of course, I may be wrong--

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:18.770
but I doubt that Jane Austen
would have quite approved

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:19.370
of the movies.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:22.610
[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:26.540
We know from Penny Gay's
Jane Austen and the Theater

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:30.170
that she was a keen theatergoer,
whenever she had the chance,

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:33.140
and was even quite
undiscriminating in her taste,

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:38.390
which ranged from Edmund Kean
as Shylock in Drury Lane in 1814

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:41.810
to the lurid melodramas of
which we find several parodies

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:45.250
among her juvenilia.

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:48.130
But there is and always
has been a titillating,

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:52.090
voyeuristic quality to the
movies, a sense of the camera's

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:56.212
inevitable intrusion into
personal and domestic privacy

00:01:56.212 --> 00:01:57.670
that must have made
moving pictures

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:01.900
more like the amateur dramatics
and amateur actors of which we

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.630
know from their treatment
in Mansfield Park

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:07.390
that she disapproved.

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:11.380
There, in arguing against the
amateur performance of Lovers'

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:16.480
Vows, a play about extramarital
sex and illegitimacy,

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:19.030
Edmund Bertram pleads
against his brother

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:21.370
Tom's contention that their
absent father would approve

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:24.220
of it because he approved of
their declaiming Shakespeare

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:26.590
when they were boys.

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:29.110
"My father wished us as
schoolboys to speak well,"

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:30.482
says Edmund.

00:02:30.482 --> 00:02:32.440
"But he would never wish
his grown-up daughters

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:34.450
to be acting plays.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:36.560
His sense of decorum is strict."

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.170
So was Jane Austen's
sense of decorum

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:43.750
strict, like that
of her alter ego,

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:46.480
Fanny Price, in the novel,
who adamantly refuses

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:48.610
to take part in the play.

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:51.490
Lionel Trilling thought this had
to do with Fanny's and probably

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:56.200
also her creator's horror
at the insincerity of anyone

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.510
like the temperamentally
inclined actor

00:02:58.510 --> 00:03:01.180
Henry Crawford, who
could relish pretending

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.840
to be someone he was not.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.710
It is the actor in him, examples
of which we see off the stage

00:03:07.710 --> 00:03:10.080
as well as on, as when
he imagines himself

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:13.710
to be a naval hero or a
spellbinding preacher,

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:15.810
that makes Fanny mistrust him.

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:18.090
And of course,
females on the stage

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:19.620
were widely and
not always wrongly

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:21.630
suspected of
unchastity for as long

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:25.590
as there had been females
on the English stage, which

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:30.527
was for just over a century
before Jane Austen was born.

00:03:30.527 --> 00:03:32.110
But I think there's
another reason why

00:03:32.110 --> 00:03:36.010
she might not have approved of
the movies or of television.

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:39.760
This lies in what I believe to
be the central paradox of Jane

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.830
Austen's novels.

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:44.950
It's certainly true that
she prized sincerity.

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:48.250
"My Emma," says Mr. Knightley
to the eponymous heroine

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:50.600
of that novel,
"does not everything

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:55.270
serve to prove more and more the
beauty of truth and sincerity

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:58.288
in all our dealings
with each other?"

00:03:58.288 --> 00:03:59.830
But there was another
virtue that she

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:02.050
prized even more highly.

00:04:02.050 --> 00:04:04.360
And that, from one
point of view, at least,

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:08.200
is the very antithesis
of sincerity.

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:12.670
And that is the virtue of
what she calls self-command.

00:04:12.670 --> 00:04:16.000
"Oh, that my dear mother had
more command over herself,"

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:18.640
laments Jane Bennet
in Pride and Prejudice

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:21.820
when her dear mother keeps
giving her painful reminders

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:26.500
of her having been forsaken, as
she supposes, by Mr. Bingley.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:29.050
Self-command was particularly
important for women

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:34.030
because the inability to
suppress her feelings of regard

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:37.360
or attachment for a man, at
least until he was prepared

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:41.080
to offer her has his hand
and fortune in marriage,

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:45.610
was likely to lead a woman into
unchastity and consequent ruin,

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:49.420
as it does Maria Bertram in
Mansfield Park and Isabella

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:52.200
Thorpe in Northanger
Abbey, and almost

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.490
does Marianne Dashwood
in Sense and Sensibility

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:58.000
and Lydia Bennett in
Pride and Prejudice.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:01.330
It may seem hard
for us to reconcile

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:03.580
such suppression
of one's feelings

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:07.240
with the truth and sincerity
in all our dealings

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.610
recommended by Mr.
Knightley, but I

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:14.380
don't think it was hard for Jane
Austen or her contemporaries.

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:17.380
Our first requirement for
appreciating her, therefore,

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:21.010
either on the page
or on the screen,

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:23.590
is for ourselves to
try to understand

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:26.110
the importance for the
kind of moral seriousness

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:28.990
of which Jane Austen is
always an advocate, of what we

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:33.760
would nowadays call the
repression of powerful emotion.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:38.610
The first time I
was asked to compare

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:41.340
the various cinematic
or televisual versions

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:44.610
of her works was in the
millennium year of 2000,

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:49.440
before many of you were born and
was at the request of something

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:53.220
called the Independent Women's
Quarterly, a publication now

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:56.160
no longer in existence,
I'm sorry to say.

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:58.650
Of the screen versions of
her novels then existing,

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:02.730
I chose the 1993 adaptation
of Sense and Sensibility,

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:05.940
written by Emma Thompson
and directed by Ang Lee,

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:07.890
as the best.

00:06:07.890 --> 00:06:10.410
You'll have a chance
to see it on Tuesday,

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:13.260
and I think I can promise
you a treat in store,

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.750
if you haven't
seen it, including

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:19.290
fine performances by Kate
Winslet as Marianne Dashwood,

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:22.110
the late Alan Rickman
as Colonel Brandon,

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:25.680
Imelda Staunton as Mrs. Palmer,
Hugh Laurie as Mr. Palmer,

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:30.270
Imogen Stubbs as Lucy Steele,
and Greg Wise as Willoughby.

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:32.670
Even Hugh Grant as Edward
Ferrars, who you would think

00:06:32.670 --> 00:06:35.070
would be all wrong
for the part, manages

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:40.050
to make the blockish Edward
almost plausibly lovable

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:40.980
by poor Eleanor--

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:43.130
[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]

00:06:43.990 --> 00:06:46.510
--who is played by
Emma Thompson herself.

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:49.860
And it was her performance
that, more than anyone else's,

00:06:49.860 --> 00:06:52.710
which was the making of
the film, in my opinion.

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:56.280
Her heroic self-command in the
face of the twin calamities

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:59.280
of her sister's indiscretions
with Willoughby and Lucy

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:02.220
Steele's entrusting her with
the un-revealable secret

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:06.030
that the man she loves is
engaged to another literally

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:08.670
keeps her family together.

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:10.830
And it is rewarded at
the end in a scene which,

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:15.510
as I wrote at the time, it is
impossible to watch unmoved,

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:18.210
when a diffident, po-faced
Edward reveals that he is not,

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:21.600
as everyone had supposed,
married to Lucy Steele.

00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:24.870
Didn't she know that Lucy had
married his brother instead?

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:27.320
Didn't everyone know it?

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:29.450
At this point, the
hitherto impassive

00:07:29.450 --> 00:07:31.370
Eleanor, rigid
with self-command,

00:07:31.370 --> 00:07:36.170
suddenly bursts into
convulsive sobs that

00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:39.320
continue as her mother and
sisters leave the room, leaving

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:48.470
Edward and his long-despaired-of
proposals with Eleanor.

00:07:48.470 --> 00:07:50.780
The whole movie,
the whole struggle

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:53.342
between Eleanor's sense
and Marianne's sensibility,

00:07:53.342 --> 00:07:54.800
the whole of Jane
Austen, in a way,

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:58.970
is present in that moment of
the epiphany, when self-command

00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:01.280
like a dam breaking
finally gives way,

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:04.390
because it can give way to
the pent-up emotion behind it.

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:10.460
I remember reading somewhere--
alas, I cannot remember where--

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:12.680
that in Jane Austen's day,
the English were looked on

00:08:12.680 --> 00:08:15.180
by the rest of Europe the way
the Italians were later to be,

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:18.174
as typically emotionally
incontinent--

00:08:18.174 --> 00:08:20.900
[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:23.090
--wearing their hearts
on their sleeves,

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:25.070
as my father used to call it--

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:27.080
and unashamed to weep in public.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:29.240
It was supposedly only
in the Victorian era

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:32.990
that the famous British stiff
upper lip came into being,

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:35.240
especially for men,
and the ability

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:37.580
to suppress one's feelings
became a marker of manhood

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:39.620
as well as politesse.

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:41.510
I'm not sure that this is true.

00:08:41.510 --> 00:08:43.760
At the first meeting in
several months of the brothers

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:48.700
Knightley in Emma, we read
that, "'How do you do, George?'

00:08:48.700 --> 00:08:51.460
And 'John, how are you?'
succeeded in the true English

00:08:51.460 --> 00:08:54.160
style, burying under a
calmness that seemed to all

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:57.430
but indifference the real
attachment which would have led

00:08:57.430 --> 00:09:00.370
either of them, if requisite,
to do everything for the good

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:02.590
of the other."

00:09:02.590 --> 00:09:05.720
Self-command is as important
for men as for women,

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:09.430
in other words, as a mark of
good breeding and good manners.

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:11.770
You may have noticed
something of this kind

00:09:11.770 --> 00:09:14.500
of emotional restraint which
was still typical of the English

00:09:14.500 --> 00:09:17.980
within living memory in the
version of Pride and Prejudice

00:09:17.980 --> 00:09:21.310
that was shown
here this afternoon

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:23.410
and which was made during
the Second World War,

00:09:23.410 --> 00:09:27.850
when British upper lips had
to be at their stiffest.

00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:30.400
The film's emotional reticence
is particularly noticeable

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:34.540
if you compare it with such
more recent versions as the 1995

00:09:34.540 --> 00:09:38.350
six-part BBC adaptation by
Andrew Davis, or the feature

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:40.750
film of 10 years later
by Joe Wright with Keira

00:09:40.750 --> 00:09:43.180
Knightley as a
feisty postfeminist

00:09:43.180 --> 00:09:45.790
Elizabeth Bennet
and Matthew Mcfadyen

00:09:45.790 --> 00:09:48.340
as a lummox-y, hangdog Darcy.

00:09:48.340 --> 00:09:51.094
[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]

00:09:53.850 --> 00:09:56.578
Laurence Olivier, the
Darcy in the 1940 version

00:09:56.578 --> 00:09:58.620
of Pride and Prejudice,
was thought, at the time,

00:09:58.620 --> 00:09:59.995
to represent
something of a break

00:09:59.995 --> 00:10:02.430
with the old declamatory
style of acting

00:10:02.430 --> 00:10:04.740
more appropriate to
the stage in favor

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:07.950
of a more realistic manner
demanded by the movies.

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:10.210
But his Darcy seems
to me to be an example

00:10:10.210 --> 00:10:11.910
not so much of
emotional suppression as

00:10:11.910 --> 00:10:14.190
of emotional absence.

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:16.020
More generally, the
film skates over all

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:18.720
the novel's emotional
depths, as we have since

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:21.060
learned to see them,
and treats it, instead,

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:24.120
is nothing but a rollicking
comedy of manners, something

00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:25.860
also evident in the
extreme liberties

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:29.600
that the Hollywood of that
period took with the text.

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:32.550
Most shocking of which, in my
view, is its making the visit

00:10:32.550 --> 00:10:36.090
of Lady Catherine de
Bourgh, Edna May Oliver,

00:10:36.090 --> 00:10:38.760
to Greer Garson's Elizabeth
on the eve of their engagement

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:42.300
to Darcy as a put-up job between
the old lady and her nephew

00:10:42.300 --> 00:10:44.790
to test the younger woman
for any mercenary motive she

00:10:44.790 --> 00:10:47.348
might have in accepting him.

00:10:47.348 --> 00:10:49.140
This doesn't even make
sense from the point

00:10:49.140 --> 00:10:51.180
of view of the plot,
since her earlier refusal

00:10:51.180 --> 00:10:53.010
of Darcy's proposal
should have put to rest

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:55.620
any suspicions of that sort.

00:10:55.620 --> 00:10:58.740
My own suspicion is that the
screenwriter, Aldous Huxley,

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:00.540
raised in
stiff-upper-lip Britain

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:02.460
and working on a
dramatization of the novel

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:06.060
by the Australian transplant
Helen Jerome, simply

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:08.280
took its theme of emotional
restraint and reserve

00:11:08.280 --> 00:11:09.597
for granted.

00:11:09.597 --> 00:11:11.430
The same is true, though,
to a lesser extent

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:15.750
if you go back and look at the
BBC'S adaptations of Persuasion

00:11:15.750 --> 00:11:20.350
and Emma from 1971 and
1972, respectively,

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:23.530
in which the staginess of
early television drama,

00:11:23.530 --> 00:11:26.050
especially in such
period pieces,

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:29.110
costume dramas such as these,
combined with what one can only

00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:32.650
supposed to be the congenital
stiffness of the actors,

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:36.220
make them almost unwatchable
by present-day standards,

00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:39.520
though they do have the
virtue of including more,

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:41.170
if it is a virtue,
including more

00:11:41.170 --> 00:11:44.650
of the text than most versions.

00:11:44.650 --> 00:11:47.110
Yet in their stiffness
and formality of manner

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:50.380
and apparent lack of
feeling, these 1970s versions

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:52.390
of Jane Austen must
be closer to the way

00:11:52.390 --> 00:11:55.660
she herself would have
envisaged her characters

00:11:55.660 --> 00:11:58.307
than later versions.

00:11:58.307 --> 00:12:00.890
The more important way in which
they go astray, in my opinion,

00:12:00.890 --> 00:12:04.240
is that they treat the novels
as being satirical in a way

00:12:04.240 --> 00:12:06.130
that Jane Austen was
not, or not in the sense

00:12:06.130 --> 00:12:08.290
as we now understand the term.

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:10.900
In other words, they see her
as a satirist of social class

00:12:10.900 --> 00:12:13.010
distinctions and
snobbery, things

00:12:13.010 --> 00:12:15.010
that were of greater
concern in the last century

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:18.280
than they were in Jane Austen's
time, more than they are now,

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:20.740
now that our political
attention has turned

00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:25.690
to more interesting classes of
the self-identified oppressed,

00:12:25.690 --> 00:12:28.360
than those who merely
lacked leisure,

00:12:28.360 --> 00:12:32.810
social prestige, or money.

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:36.110
But Jane Austen, I think, has
no political axes to grind.

00:12:36.110 --> 00:12:38.827
She takes distinctions of
social class for granted.

00:12:38.827 --> 00:12:40.910
And it would never occur
to her to ridicule others

00:12:40.910 --> 00:12:42.860
who do the same.

00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:45.650
Though some may give too much
importance to social standing,

00:12:45.650 --> 00:12:48.650
like Lady Russell in
Persuasion, of whom it is

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:51.408
said that she had "a value
for rank and consequence

00:12:51.408 --> 00:12:53.450
which blinded her a little
to the faults of those

00:12:53.450 --> 00:12:55.610
who possess them."

00:12:55.610 --> 00:12:58.700
Corin Redgrave of the Socialist
Workers' Party and the famously

00:12:58.700 --> 00:13:01.040
radical Redgrave
family, hamming it up

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:04.640
as the snobbish Sir Walter
Elliot in the 1995 Persuasion,

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:07.550
so much so that he's almost
allowed to overshadow the love

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:09.890
story of Anne and
Captain Wentworth,

00:13:09.890 --> 00:13:12.920
may have been the last gasp of
this mid-century view of Jane

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:16.970
Austen as social satirist.

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:18.950
But the sudden spate of
cinematic adaptations

00:13:18.950 --> 00:13:23.720
of her novels in the 1990s
and after the 20th century's

00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:26.990
obsession with social class
had largely spent itself,

00:13:26.990 --> 00:13:30.740
was the result, I believe, of a
renewed interest in Jane Austen

00:13:30.740 --> 00:13:34.732
the moralist, which is also how
she was seen in her own time.

00:13:34.732 --> 00:13:36.440
This is important
because there has never

00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:38.357
been a time in the
English-speaking world when

00:13:38.357 --> 00:13:40.910
we've been more in
need of moral guidance,

00:13:40.910 --> 00:13:44.330
now that we have a new
ideologically based morality

00:13:44.330 --> 00:13:48.110
every day further displacing
the traditional kind, the Jane

00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:52.940
Austen kind, and assuming
cultural dominance over us.

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:55.280
The linguist and social
philosopher John McWhorter

00:13:55.280 --> 00:13:58.970
does not scruple to call this
so-called "woke consciousness"

00:13:58.970 --> 00:14:02.150
a new religion.

00:14:02.150 --> 00:14:05.180
I would prefer to
call it an ideology.

00:14:05.180 --> 00:14:07.820
And the intellectual
attraction of ideology,

00:14:07.820 --> 00:14:11.840
unlike that of the religion,
which as you may remember used

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:14.420
to tell us that we
were all sinners,

00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:17.988
is that if you only believe,
you can never be wrong.

00:14:17.988 --> 00:14:20.030
The ideologue will allow
you to speak of yourself

00:14:20.030 --> 00:14:22.875
as being on the
right side of history

00:14:22.875 --> 00:14:24.500
because you may assume
that you already

00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:27.980
know how history will come out.

00:14:27.980 --> 00:14:30.560
Your ideology informs
you of this in advance.

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:32.240
And you can use your
supposed knowledge

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:35.630
of your support your supposed
rightness about everything

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:38.270
to shame those who are
still among the doubters.

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:40.973
You can tell them, as the actor
Sean Penn did at the Oscars

00:14:40.973 --> 00:14:42.890
a few years ago, that
their grandchildren will

00:14:42.890 --> 00:14:45.080
be ashamed of them
for having resisted

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:49.070
the tide of moral certainty
that must certainly carry all

00:14:49.070 --> 00:14:50.510
before it into the future.

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:55.770
In other words, our
ideologically-dominated society

00:14:55.770 --> 00:15:00.600
is as morally primitive as it
is scientifically advanced.

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:03.472
Even non-ideologues tend to
take the view, as who would not

00:15:03.472 --> 00:15:05.430
who had never been told
that there was anything

00:15:05.430 --> 00:15:10.080
to stop them, that both
morality and science are all

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:13.810
about being right, when
they both are much,

00:15:13.810 --> 00:15:16.290
much more about being wrong?

00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:18.300
We pull our morality,
like our science,

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:21.720
around us as if they were
a cloak of invulnerability

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:23.160
for our opinions.

00:15:23.160 --> 00:15:25.800
When properly understood,
both are instead

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:28.920
the instruments of those
opinion's dissections

00:15:28.920 --> 00:15:32.460
and very often, destruction.

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:34.500
Some years ago, the
journalist Kathryn Schulz

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:37.710
wrote a book called
Being Wrong: Adventures

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:40.740
in the Margin of Error,
in which she invented

00:15:40.740 --> 00:15:44.520
a new science of wrongology.

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:46.860
I recommend it to you as one
of the most important books

00:15:46.860 --> 00:15:48.810
of our century.

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:52.590
But Jane Austen was
there ahead of her.

00:15:52.590 --> 00:15:56.370
As I see them, and
as I hope you will,

00:15:56.370 --> 00:15:58.260
her novels all focus
on a moral moment

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:01.140
that can only be
called dramatic, which

00:16:01.140 --> 00:16:06.620
is why they're adapted so well
to the screen in our time,

00:16:06.620 --> 00:16:08.630
and why they have taken
on a new relevance

00:16:08.630 --> 00:16:11.180
and cogency to so many in the
very, very different world

00:16:11.180 --> 00:16:13.100
of two centuries later.

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:17.270
This is what I call
the I was wrong moment.

00:16:17.270 --> 00:16:19.340
All of her heroines
and most of her heroes

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:22.310
have to suffer through the
mortification of an I was wrong

00:16:22.310 --> 00:16:23.600
moment.

00:16:23.600 --> 00:16:26.990
Fanny Price and Anne Elliot are
partial exceptions, but only

00:16:26.990 --> 00:16:28.160
partial.

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:31.010
Although Fanny is never
far from self-doubt,

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:33.690
she is only ever wrong
about being wrong,

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:35.690
which is to say she's
wrong about her occasional

00:16:35.690 --> 00:16:39.110
doubtful I was wrong moments and
right about the Crawfords when

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:41.780
everyone else is
telling her she's wrong.

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:44.660
She's right, too,
about Maria and Julia's

00:16:44.660 --> 00:16:47.510
faults of immodesty and
right about who Edmund really

00:16:47.510 --> 00:16:50.360
loves, though she tries
hardest to talk herself

00:16:50.360 --> 00:16:52.880
into being wrong about this.

00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:54.980
It is Edmund who has
to pass through the I

00:16:54.980 --> 00:16:57.680
was wrong fire most scorchingly.

00:16:57.680 --> 00:16:59.180
And that, I think,
robs this novel

00:16:59.180 --> 00:17:00.805
of some of the
poignancy of the others,

00:17:00.805 --> 00:17:04.069
wherein female wrongness
seems to me, by contrast,

00:17:04.069 --> 00:17:06.710
to carry with it the conviction
of personal experience.

00:17:09.369 --> 00:17:12.730
Anne Elliot's I was
wrong moment takes place

00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:15.280
before the action
of the novel begins,

00:17:15.280 --> 00:17:17.589
when she realizes that she
was wrong to have yielded

00:17:17.589 --> 00:17:19.510
to Lady Russell's
persuasion in breaking off

00:17:19.510 --> 00:17:23.050
her engagement to Captain
Wentworth, which means that it

00:17:23.050 --> 00:17:26.440
is Wentworth who experiences
Persuasion's main I was

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:30.580
wrong moment in realizing after
Louisa Musgrove's accident

00:17:30.580 --> 00:17:33.610
that the firmness of purpose,
which Louisa has and Anne is

00:17:33.610 --> 00:17:37.210
supposed to lack, is not, after
all, unleavened by prudence,

00:17:37.210 --> 00:17:39.700
the most desirable
quality in a woman,

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:41.410
and that it is only
his own pride which

00:17:41.410 --> 00:17:44.800
has kept him from returning
to his first love.

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:47.770
Here, as in Mansfield
Park, the main I

00:17:47.770 --> 00:17:49.360
was wrong moments
being predicated

00:17:49.360 --> 00:17:51.580
of her principal male
characters, makes

00:17:51.580 --> 00:17:54.220
the dramatizations
of both novels,

00:17:54.220 --> 00:17:57.010
compared with the others,
less successful, in my view,

00:17:57.010 --> 00:18:00.310
perhaps, because it's less
unexpected when a man turns out

00:18:00.310 --> 00:18:00.880
to be wrong--

00:18:00.880 --> 00:18:04.312
[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]

00:18:04.312 --> 00:18:05.770
--or at least when
he admits to it.

00:18:05.770 --> 00:18:08.800
[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]

00:18:08.800 --> 00:18:13.270
This may also be why
Patricia Rozema, in her 1999

00:18:13.270 --> 00:18:15.670
version of Mansfield
Park, felt she

00:18:15.670 --> 00:18:18.160
had to jazz up Jane with
a completely gratuitous

00:18:18.160 --> 00:18:21.190
polemic against slavery
on Sir Thomas Bertram's

00:18:21.190 --> 00:18:24.010
plantations in Antigua,
something about which

00:18:24.010 --> 00:18:26.410
her Fanny, played
by Frances O'Connor,

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:30.850
obviously finds it as easy to
be right as we do in retrospect.

00:18:30.850 --> 00:18:33.760
And the same goes for the many
little feminist applause lines

00:18:33.760 --> 00:18:35.920
that even the best of the
movie and TV adaptations

00:18:35.920 --> 00:18:39.472
can't resist adding
occasionally to the original.

00:18:39.472 --> 00:18:40.930
I've already
mentioned the mistake,

00:18:40.930 --> 00:18:43.990
as I see it, in the 1995
version of Persuasion's implied

00:18:43.990 --> 00:18:46.420
self-congratulation
by concentrating

00:18:46.420 --> 00:18:48.760
too much on the obvious but
never acknowledged wrongness

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:52.030
of Sir Walter, as opposed to the
less obvious but more painfully

00:18:52.030 --> 00:18:56.530
realized wrongness
of Captain Wentworth.

00:18:56.530 --> 00:18:59.170
Sir Walter is just one of the
many characters in the novels

00:18:59.170 --> 00:19:03.070
who serve as a foil
or as foils to the I

00:19:03.070 --> 00:19:06.370
was wrong characters by being
like today's ideologues,

00:19:06.370 --> 00:19:08.260
incapable of seeing
themselves in the wrong,

00:19:08.260 --> 00:19:10.960
no matter how wrong they are.

00:19:10.960 --> 00:19:12.730
Not all these
characters are villains

00:19:12.730 --> 00:19:15.220
like Sir Walter, or General
Tilney, or William Elliott,

00:19:15.220 --> 00:19:16.690
or the Crawfords.

00:19:16.690 --> 00:19:19.660
Some only come in for more
or less gentle ridicule,

00:19:19.660 --> 00:19:24.040
like Mr. Collins, or Mrs.
Elton, or Mrs. Bennet.

00:19:24.040 --> 00:19:28.330
But what they all have in
common is self-assurance.

00:19:28.330 --> 00:19:31.030
Self-assurance is
self-command, but

00:19:31.030 --> 00:19:33.160
without that other
great Austenian virtue

00:19:33.160 --> 00:19:35.470
of self-knowledge.

00:19:35.470 --> 00:19:37.810
Unaware of itself,
self-assurance

00:19:37.810 --> 00:19:39.820
takes its own
mannerly insincerity

00:19:39.820 --> 00:19:41.980
for the warmest
sympathy, or even, as

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:45.070
in Henry Crawford's case, love.

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:47.110
Therefore, it is immune
to the self-doubt that

00:19:47.110 --> 00:19:50.560
is the trial through which all
Jane Austen's heroines have

00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:53.420
to pass.

00:19:53.420 --> 00:19:57.530
Nowadays, we might call
such self-assurance "cool."

00:19:57.530 --> 00:20:00.770
Henry Crawford is
certainly a very cool guy.

00:20:00.770 --> 00:20:03.770
So is Mr. William
Elliot in Persuasion.

00:20:03.770 --> 00:20:07.070
John Thorpe of Northanger
Abbey is a mere buffoon,

00:20:07.070 --> 00:20:08.360
like so many Oxford men--

00:20:08.360 --> 00:20:11.141
[AUDIENCE LAUGHS]

00:20:11.141 --> 00:20:14.630
--trying, without
success, to be a cool guy.

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:17.090
But all of them share what
Jane Austen herself most often

00:20:17.090 --> 00:20:20.390
refers to as
self-conceit or vanity,

00:20:20.390 --> 00:20:23.240
not that is Sir Walter's
kind of personal vanity

00:20:23.240 --> 00:20:25.160
of personal
appearance so much as

00:20:25.160 --> 00:20:27.530
that which she attributes
to Henry Crawford

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:30.950
when he is said to have "had
all the disposition to persevere

00:20:30.950 --> 00:20:32.750
that Sir Thomas could wish him."

00:20:32.750 --> 00:20:36.170
He had vanity, which strongly
inclined him in the first place

00:20:36.170 --> 00:20:37.810
to think that
Fanny did love him,

00:20:37.810 --> 00:20:39.435
although she might
not know it herself.

00:20:42.450 --> 00:20:44.670
This kind of vanity is
the salient characteristic

00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:47.550
of all her villains and
her objects of ridicule,

00:20:47.550 --> 00:20:51.780
both the cool and the uncool,
like Mr. Collins in Pride

00:20:51.780 --> 00:20:55.003
and Prejudice or
Mr. Elton in Emma,

00:20:55.003 --> 00:20:57.420
the two latter being clergymen,
interestingly, rather than

00:20:57.420 --> 00:20:59.670
actors.

00:20:59.670 --> 00:21:04.080
Frank Churchill of Emma
is also a very cool guy.

00:21:04.080 --> 00:21:08.680
And so, as Mr. Knightley
points out, not to be trusted.

00:21:08.680 --> 00:21:11.490
But he escapes absolute villainy
by sufficient self-knowledge

00:21:11.490 --> 00:21:13.380
to be always aware of
his putting on an act

00:21:13.380 --> 00:21:16.440
and of having a very
particular object in doing so.

00:21:16.440 --> 00:21:20.190
And also by knowing, even as he
does so, that putting on an act

00:21:20.190 --> 00:21:21.680
is wrong.

00:21:21.680 --> 00:21:24.510
He is maybe the only character
in the Jane Austen corpus who

00:21:24.510 --> 00:21:27.750
is both culpably
self-assured and capable

00:21:27.750 --> 00:21:31.050
of a sincerely penitential
I was wrong moment,

00:21:31.050 --> 00:21:33.540
of at least partial
self-redemption.

00:21:33.540 --> 00:21:34.950
Darcy might be
another candidate,

00:21:34.950 --> 00:21:38.850
except that his self-assurance
is less culpable and less

00:21:38.850 --> 00:21:42.270
calculating and his confession
of error more sincere.

00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:47.500
Willoughby in Sense
and Sensibility

00:21:47.500 --> 00:21:51.910
is an interesting
case in this respect,

00:21:51.910 --> 00:21:58.720
in a scene which was, for
understandable reasons,

00:21:58.720 --> 00:22:03.940
cut from the movie,
we will see him

00:22:03.940 --> 00:22:07.300
appearing before Eleanor
penitent about his treatment

00:22:07.300 --> 00:22:10.480
of Marianne and
pleading with her

00:22:10.480 --> 00:22:12.590
for a measure of forgiveness.

00:22:12.590 --> 00:22:16.780
This scene is obviously
meant to be taken as sincere

00:22:16.780 --> 00:22:19.270
on his part, but he has
not the self-knowledge

00:22:19.270 --> 00:22:23.080
of Claudius in Hamlet who,
in his penitential scene,

00:22:23.080 --> 00:22:25.390
asks himself, "May one
be pardoned and retain

00:22:25.390 --> 00:22:26.860
the offense?"

00:22:26.860 --> 00:22:29.500
He is, unfortunately,
still possessed

00:22:29.500 --> 00:22:33.970
of those effects, a rich wife
and an end to indebtedness

00:22:33.970 --> 00:22:37.300
as well as a reconciliation
with his aunt, for which he

00:22:37.300 --> 00:22:39.880
jilted Marianne.

00:22:39.880 --> 00:22:44.050
His regret, what he had to
give up for them, having been

00:22:44.050 --> 00:22:47.472
insufficient to prevent him
from taking the moral course,

00:22:47.472 --> 00:22:49.180
has become something
he will have to live

00:22:49.180 --> 00:22:51.010
with for the rest of his life.

00:22:51.010 --> 00:22:52.720
The crime, as Eleanor
observes, carries

00:22:52.720 --> 00:22:54.460
with it its own punishment.

00:22:54.460 --> 00:22:57.190
All this the movie
reduces to a single shot

00:22:57.190 --> 00:22:59.680
at the very end of Willoughby,
all alone on horseback,

00:22:59.680 --> 00:23:02.500
looking sadly down from a
nearby hill on the scene

00:23:02.500 --> 00:23:03.910
of the double wedding.

00:23:06.790 --> 00:23:09.280
Just as self-assurance
is characteristic of all

00:23:09.280 --> 00:23:11.170
the bad or ridiculous
people in her novels,

00:23:11.170 --> 00:23:13.570
so is self-doubt and
self-correction characteristic

00:23:13.570 --> 00:23:17.080
of all her heroes and
especially her heroines.

00:23:17.080 --> 00:23:19.390
Her portrayal of her
unerring instinct

00:23:19.390 --> 00:23:21.880
for what the Greeks
called the peripeteia,

00:23:21.880 --> 00:23:24.610
or reversal in the dramatic
I was wrong moment,

00:23:24.610 --> 00:23:27.940
is what makes her novels, as
well as the best of the movies

00:23:27.940 --> 00:23:30.430
or miniseries based
on them, peculiarly

00:23:30.430 --> 00:23:33.150
relevant to our time.

00:23:33.150 --> 00:23:35.290
I think it's no coincidence
that the two most often

00:23:35.290 --> 00:23:38.350
and most successfully adapted
novels, Pride and Prejudice

00:23:38.350 --> 00:23:41.710
and Emma, are also the two with
the most and the most affecting

00:23:41.710 --> 00:23:43.960
I was wrong moments.

00:23:43.960 --> 00:23:45.760
The final chapters of
Pride and Prejudice

00:23:45.760 --> 00:23:48.220
are a positive
riot of wrongness,

00:23:48.220 --> 00:23:50.710
beginning with Jane's
being wrong about Bingley's

00:23:50.710 --> 00:23:52.630
continuing and
constant regard for her

00:23:52.630 --> 00:23:55.720
and Bingley's being wrong
about her lack of same for him.

00:23:55.720 --> 00:23:57.430
But this is nothing
next to Elizabeth

00:23:57.430 --> 00:23:59.740
constantly trying to talk
herself into being wrong,

00:23:59.740 --> 00:24:01.900
for the second time, but
in the opposite direction,

00:24:01.900 --> 00:24:04.870
about Darcy's feelings for
her, until she discovers

00:24:04.870 --> 00:24:07.450
that she was wrong about
being wrong from Darcy's

00:24:07.450 --> 00:24:10.000
confession to her about all
the ways he was wrong until she

00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:13.450
herself put him right.

00:24:13.450 --> 00:24:15.947
Then, when both daughters
and their fiancés have sorted

00:24:15.947 --> 00:24:18.280
the right from the wrong,
they have to put their family,

00:24:18.280 --> 00:24:21.310
father, mother, sisters, right
about the ways they have been

00:24:21.310 --> 00:24:23.425
wrong about Lizzie's
supposed hatred of Darcy.

00:24:26.010 --> 00:24:28.500
All this without mentioning
the superlative wrongness

00:24:28.500 --> 00:24:29.550
of Catherine Bingley--

00:24:29.550 --> 00:24:31.110
or Caroline Bingley's, sorry--

00:24:31.110 --> 00:24:34.590
lady Catherine De Bourgh,
and Mr. Collins, none of whom

00:24:34.590 --> 00:24:38.700
has the capacity to recognize
his or her own wrongness.

00:24:38.700 --> 00:24:41.070
Not a character escapes
the pitfalls of wrongness

00:24:41.070 --> 00:24:42.690
of one sort or
another, except perhaps

00:24:42.690 --> 00:24:44.378
for Charlotte Lucas,
who is presumed

00:24:44.378 --> 00:24:46.920
to have gone into her marriage
with Mr. Collins with her eyes

00:24:46.920 --> 00:24:49.890
open to his many and
varied wrongnessess,

00:24:49.890 --> 00:24:51.934
and closed to them thereafter.

00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:56.640
And all the characters
are defined for us

00:24:56.640 --> 00:24:58.560
by their reaction
to being wrong,

00:24:58.560 --> 00:25:01.170
even when, as in the cases
of Mrs. Bennett, Lydia,

00:25:01.170 --> 00:25:03.900
and Wickham, they can never
be got to acknowledge it.

00:25:03.900 --> 00:25:05.750
Mrs. Bennett simply
ignores her own mistakes

00:25:05.750 --> 00:25:07.500
when they are revealed
to her, and assumes

00:25:07.500 --> 00:25:10.890
she's always thought of Bingley
and Darcy as she does now.

00:25:10.890 --> 00:25:13.350
In this she is like Mrs.
Allen in Northanger Abbey

00:25:13.350 --> 00:25:15.510
and Mr. Weston in
Emma, both of whom

00:25:15.510 --> 00:25:17.760
keep saying I knew
it would be so,

00:25:17.760 --> 00:25:20.080
when everything they
had said would be so

00:25:20.080 --> 00:25:22.950
turns out to be wrong.

00:25:22.950 --> 00:25:24.930
Mr. Bennett is a
particularly interesting case

00:25:24.930 --> 00:25:27.180
in this respect, as
he is unusually ready

00:25:27.180 --> 00:25:30.300
to admit to being wrong, but
at the same time feels it

00:25:30.300 --> 00:25:33.300
less, or less lastingly,
than anyone else in the novel

00:25:33.300 --> 00:25:35.100
with that capacity.

00:25:35.100 --> 00:25:37.320
In confessing to his
own fault and the laxity

00:25:37.320 --> 00:25:40.080
of parental control, which has
resulted in Lydia's elopement

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:42.330
with Wickham, he
says to Elizabeth,

00:25:42.330 --> 00:25:44.490
"Who should suffer but myself?

00:25:44.490 --> 00:25:47.035
It has been my own doing
and I ought to feel it."

00:25:47.035 --> 00:25:51.450
"You must not be too severe
upon yourself," Lizzie replies.

00:25:51.450 --> 00:25:54.300
"You may well warn me against
such an evil," he says.

00:25:54.300 --> 00:25:57.330
"Human nature is so
prone to fall into it.

00:25:57.330 --> 00:25:58.650
No, Lizzie.

00:25:58.650 --> 00:26:02.010
Let me once in my life feel
how much I have been to blame.

00:26:02.010 --> 00:26:04.980
I'm not afraid of being
overpowered by the impression.

00:26:04.980 --> 00:26:06.450
It will pass away soon enough."

00:26:09.310 --> 00:26:11.290
The comedy of
interlacing wrongnessess

00:26:11.290 --> 00:26:13.930
in these final chapters and
the almost farcical quality

00:26:13.930 --> 00:26:16.960
of our delight in Miss
Austen's unraveling of them

00:26:16.960 --> 00:26:20.083
to make everything turn
out incredibly right

00:26:20.083 --> 00:26:22.750
must be why this has always been
the most popular of her novels,

00:26:22.750 --> 00:26:26.500
and why its various adaptations
have been on the whole

00:26:26.500 --> 00:26:28.690
the most successful.

00:26:28.690 --> 00:26:32.620
The best of these has still
got to be that 1995 BBC

00:26:32.620 --> 00:26:35.980
version in six parts
with Colin Firth as Darcy

00:26:35.980 --> 00:26:40.240
and Jennifer Ehle as Elizabeth,
not to mention the great Alison

00:26:40.240 --> 00:26:45.040
Steadman as the Mrs. Bennett,
and all Misses Bennets.

00:26:45.040 --> 00:26:47.080
But the connoisseur
of wrongness,

00:26:47.080 --> 00:26:49.750
which I hope you are all
beginning to be by now,

00:26:49.750 --> 00:26:52.600
must always have a
special place in his heart

00:26:52.600 --> 00:26:57.130
for Emma, since Emma Woodhouse
is by far the wrongest of all

00:26:57.130 --> 00:26:59.950
her heroines, more wrong
even than the hopelessly

00:26:59.950 --> 00:27:01.720
naive and four years
younger Catherine

00:27:01.720 --> 00:27:04.180
Moreland in Northanger
Abbey, at least half

00:27:04.180 --> 00:27:07.360
of whose wrongness
passes unnoticed by her,

00:27:07.360 --> 00:27:09.610
and remains a private
joke between the author

00:27:09.610 --> 00:27:10.300
and her readers.

00:27:13.020 --> 00:27:15.540
Emma hasn't Catherine's
excuse for going wrong,

00:27:15.540 --> 00:27:18.030
which may be why Jane Austen
thought that nobody would much

00:27:18.030 --> 00:27:20.460
like her but herself.

00:27:20.460 --> 00:27:22.830
The number of adaptations
of the novel in recent years

00:27:22.830 --> 00:27:24.510
suggests that she
was wrong about this.

00:27:27.360 --> 00:27:30.150
But is that because we like her?

00:27:30.150 --> 00:27:34.170
Do we like her because or in
spite of her numerous errors?

00:27:34.170 --> 00:27:37.080
Let's try to count them up.

00:27:37.080 --> 00:27:40.410
First, as Mr. Knightley points
out to her in chapter one,

00:27:40.410 --> 00:27:41.850
she's wrong to
think that she has

00:27:41.850 --> 00:27:44.940
arranged the match between
Mr. Weston and Miss Taylor.

00:27:44.940 --> 00:27:47.910
Then she's wrong about
Harriet and Robert Martin,

00:27:47.910 --> 00:27:49.800
wrong about Harriet
and Mr. Elton,

00:27:49.800 --> 00:27:51.690
wrong about Harriet
and Frank Churchill.

00:27:51.690 --> 00:27:53.940
Wrong finally about
Harriet and Mr. Knightley.

00:27:53.940 --> 00:27:57.240
And each of these major errors
comprises numerous lesser ones,

00:27:57.240 --> 00:27:59.220
like all those
instances of which

00:27:59.220 --> 00:28:02.310
she is so eager to mistake
Mr. Elton's little attentions

00:28:02.310 --> 00:28:07.240
to Harriet before her eyes are
opened as to their real object.

00:28:07.240 --> 00:28:10.540
And then there are her mistakes
about Frank Churchill herself,

00:28:10.540 --> 00:28:13.585
about Jane Fairfax and
Mr. Dixon, Jane Fairfax

00:28:13.585 --> 00:28:15.460
and the mysterious
present of the pianoforte,

00:28:15.460 --> 00:28:18.700
and most momentously, but like
everybody else in Highbury,

00:28:18.700 --> 00:28:21.550
her being wrong about
Jane and Frank Churchill--

00:28:21.550 --> 00:28:23.590
part of what leads her
to be wrong in her insult

00:28:23.590 --> 00:28:26.350
to Miss Bates, a mistake
also pointed out to her

00:28:26.350 --> 00:28:28.740
by Mr. Knightley.

00:28:28.740 --> 00:28:30.780
His and the Westons
being wrong about her

00:28:30.780 --> 00:28:32.580
and Frank Churchill
appears a mere trifle

00:28:32.580 --> 00:28:35.790
next to the number of Emma's
wrong thoughts and deeds,

00:28:35.790 --> 00:28:39.060
every one of which she has to
face up to sooner or later.

00:28:39.060 --> 00:28:41.430
Though her final mistake
about Mr. Knightley's feelings

00:28:41.430 --> 00:28:44.100
for her are as happily
wrong as Lizzie Bennet's

00:28:44.100 --> 00:28:47.870
about Mr. Darcy's
feelings for her.

00:28:47.870 --> 00:28:49.790
Actually there is
one species of error

00:28:49.790 --> 00:28:51.860
that she's not shown
as facing up to,

00:28:51.860 --> 00:28:55.310
though it may be that we are
meant to understand she does

00:28:55.310 --> 00:28:56.660
as it takes place in private.

00:28:56.660 --> 00:28:58.430
Perhaps her
self-correction is allowed

00:28:58.430 --> 00:29:00.830
to take place off the page.

00:29:00.830 --> 00:29:03.200
But when she rages to
herself about Mr. Elton's

00:29:03.200 --> 00:29:06.470
misdirected proposal,
she abuses him to herself

00:29:06.470 --> 00:29:08.120
for exactly the
fault of aspiring

00:29:08.120 --> 00:29:12.860
to rise above his
social level that he

00:29:12.860 --> 00:29:15.290
had abused Harriet Smith for.

00:29:15.290 --> 00:29:17.900
So much to her annoyance.

00:29:17.900 --> 00:29:20.990
This, in turn, reminds us of
her snobbery towards Mr. Martin,

00:29:20.990 --> 00:29:23.480
though it does
not so remind her.

00:29:23.480 --> 00:29:26.690
She still thinks she was
right to break off that match.

00:29:26.690 --> 00:29:28.940
The failure here is
one of self-awareness,

00:29:28.940 --> 00:29:30.830
of not being able to
see when she's blaming

00:29:30.830 --> 00:29:34.740
others for her own faults.

00:29:34.740 --> 00:29:37.020
The dawning of
self-awareness is the rarest

00:29:37.020 --> 00:29:41.460
and most spiritually significant
of all I was wrong moments,

00:29:41.460 --> 00:29:44.610
but in Emma it takes
its most benign form

00:29:44.610 --> 00:29:46.770
when after Harriet
confesses what

00:29:46.770 --> 00:29:49.590
she takes to be, wrongly
of course, an attachment

00:29:49.590 --> 00:29:52.440
to Mr. Knightley, it
darted through her

00:29:52.440 --> 00:29:54.780
with the speed of an arrow
that Mr. Knightley must

00:29:54.780 --> 00:29:58.290
marry no one but herself.

00:29:58.290 --> 00:30:00.180
Here at last we are
told is the beginning

00:30:00.180 --> 00:30:02.520
of that knowledge
of herself that

00:30:02.520 --> 00:30:06.155
can only come with a recognition
that she has been, quote,

00:30:06.155 --> 00:30:07.950
"totally ignorant
of her own heart."

00:30:10.490 --> 00:30:14.720
So which is the best
screen version of Emma?

00:30:14.720 --> 00:30:17.060
There is much to be said
for Gwyneth Paltrow as Emma

00:30:17.060 --> 00:30:20.130
in Douglas McGrath's
movie of 1996, which

00:30:20.130 --> 00:30:24.800
you may see tomorrow, but
it goes badly wrong itself

00:30:24.800 --> 00:30:27.950
in taking one of Emma's
wrongnesses away from her

00:30:27.950 --> 00:30:30.200
and giving it to Frank
Churchill instead.

00:30:30.200 --> 00:30:33.110
This is the suspicion about
Jane Fairfax and Mr. Dixon,

00:30:33.110 --> 00:30:39.380
which in this version is
proposed by Frank instead

00:30:39.380 --> 00:30:43.340
of being Emma's
surmise, which is out

00:30:43.340 --> 00:30:45.380
of her natural love of intrigue.

00:30:48.095 --> 00:30:49.220
In other words, it's a lie.

00:30:56.300 --> 00:30:58.550
More seriously,
Miss Paltrow's I was

00:30:58.550 --> 00:31:01.100
wrong moments are almost
as anodyne as those

00:31:01.100 --> 00:31:04.370
of her alter ego in Amy
Heckerling's Clueless, which

00:31:04.370 --> 00:31:06.620
came out at about the same time.

00:31:06.620 --> 00:31:09.620
There, the Emma character
called Cher, and played

00:31:09.620 --> 00:31:13.550
by Alicia Silverstone,
makes only comic errors,

00:31:13.550 --> 00:31:15.770
while her very brief
I was wrong moment

00:31:15.770 --> 00:31:18.470
is smoothed away and
rendered unimportant

00:31:18.470 --> 00:31:22.160
by the emollient words of a
very implausible Mr. Knightley

00:31:22.160 --> 00:31:26.840
character played by
the young Paul Rudd.

00:31:26.840 --> 00:31:29.228
Miss Paltrow's Emma may
say that she's wrong,

00:31:29.228 --> 00:31:30.770
but we never really
see anything that

00:31:30.770 --> 00:31:33.405
looks very much like a
dart of self-knowledge

00:31:33.405 --> 00:31:35.030
hitting her with the
speed of an arrow.

00:31:37.830 --> 00:31:40.860
On the plus side, Ewan McGregor
makes a very plausibly caddish

00:31:40.860 --> 00:31:45.240
Frank Churchill, Alan Cumming
is the most comically unctuous

00:31:45.240 --> 00:31:48.210
of all possible Mr. Eltons,
and Jeremy Northam the most

00:31:48.210 --> 00:31:52.230
Mr. Knightley-like Mr.
Knightley that I have seen.

00:31:52.230 --> 00:31:55.140
Toni Collette has in some ways
the most interesting Harriet,

00:31:55.140 --> 00:31:57.240
too, but she's too
much over the top,

00:31:57.240 --> 00:32:00.060
like an embryo Mrs.
Bennett, and not

00:32:00.060 --> 00:32:02.610
a plausible love interest
for any of the suitors

00:32:02.610 --> 00:32:06.360
to whom Emma would assign her,
or even the allegedly sensible

00:32:06.360 --> 00:32:08.730
Robert Martin.

00:32:08.730 --> 00:32:11.070
The most beautiful, as
Harriet is described

00:32:11.070 --> 00:32:15.150
in the novel, as well as the
most charmingly dim Harriet I

00:32:15.150 --> 00:32:19.020
have seen is Louise Dylan in
the 2009 BBC miniseries, which

00:32:19.020 --> 00:32:23.310
also has by far the best Mr.
Woodhouse in Michael Gambon,

00:32:23.310 --> 00:32:26.370
and a very decent Emma
and Knightley in Romola

00:32:26.370 --> 00:32:28.560
Garai and Johnny Lee Miller.

00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:30.450
Like all mini-series
it also has room

00:32:30.450 --> 00:32:32.040
to squeeze in far
more of the novel

00:32:32.040 --> 00:32:36.500
and all its riches than
a feature film can do.

00:32:36.500 --> 00:32:38.720
As for the last
year's film of Emma

00:32:38.720 --> 00:32:42.930
directed by Autumn deWilde,
I hated almost everything

00:32:42.930 --> 00:32:43.430
about it.

00:32:46.640 --> 00:32:50.360
Beginning with the wide-eyed,
gamine-like, almost babydoll

00:32:50.360 --> 00:32:53.270
look of Anya Taylor Joy's Emma.

00:32:53.270 --> 00:32:56.300
Indeed, I hated the looks of
all the main characters except

00:32:56.300 --> 00:32:58.040
for Miranda Hart's
Miss Bates, who

00:32:58.040 --> 00:33:01.340
was perhaps the best
in that role I've seen.

00:33:01.340 --> 00:33:04.190
I also hated the way Bill
Nighy's Mr. Woodhouse enters

00:33:04.190 --> 00:33:07.130
leaping, as if to stick
a thumb in the eye

00:33:07.130 --> 00:33:10.280
of every other portrayal of
him as a valetudinarian, as in,

00:33:10.280 --> 00:33:11.330
you know, the novel.

00:33:14.600 --> 00:33:18.350
I hated the way the supposedly
manly Mr. Knightley, age 37,

00:33:18.350 --> 00:33:20.720
played by 37-year-old
Johnny Flynn,

00:33:20.720 --> 00:33:23.900
acts like a lovesick teenager,
pumping his fist in the air

00:33:23.900 --> 00:33:26.210
and showing his apparent
lack of self-command

00:33:26.210 --> 00:33:29.180
in every emotion
he openly feels.

00:33:29.180 --> 00:33:31.820
Not at all, in other words,
in the true English style

00:33:31.820 --> 00:33:34.340
as described by Jane Austen.

00:33:34.340 --> 00:33:36.440
I hated the self-conscious
exaggeration

00:33:36.440 --> 00:33:40.100
of every comic effect, and the
obtrusive musical pointing,

00:33:40.100 --> 00:33:43.430
with a jumble of Irish-sounding
folk music, choral church

00:33:43.430 --> 00:33:48.140
music, and bits of Haydn,
Mozart, and Beethoven.

00:33:48.140 --> 00:33:52.280
But when it comes
to the wrongology

00:33:52.280 --> 00:33:54.770
of Emma's various
self-epiphanies,

00:33:54.770 --> 00:33:57.980
especially when she responds
to Mr. Knightley proposal,

00:33:57.980 --> 00:34:00.980
with a look of one absolutely
poleaxed with dawning

00:34:00.980 --> 00:34:05.420
self-awareness, her almost
comically wide-set eyes finally

00:34:05.420 --> 00:34:06.350
coming into their own.

00:34:11.270 --> 00:34:16.190
Moreover, a spontaneous
nosebleed on the occasion

00:34:16.190 --> 00:34:18.290
seems no more than an
appropriate reminder

00:34:18.290 --> 00:34:21.710
of the wounding and
death and rebirth

00:34:21.710 --> 00:34:24.469
that the penitential
process mimics.

00:34:24.469 --> 00:34:27.739
Not to mention the arrow
of self-awareness darting

00:34:27.739 --> 00:34:30.949
through her, as I
mentioned earlier.

00:34:30.949 --> 00:34:35.272
This, the greatest of the
cinematic I was wrong moments,

00:34:35.272 --> 00:34:37.730
is good enough to make me think
that all those other things

00:34:37.730 --> 00:34:40.580
that I disliked about the
movie might be deliberate

00:34:40.580 --> 00:34:44.639
exercises in
wrongology themselves.

00:34:44.639 --> 00:34:49.400
There's no doubt, at any
rate, that I too can be wrong.

00:34:49.400 --> 00:34:50.852
Thank you.

00:34:50.852 --> 00:34:53.282
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:02.875 --> 00:35:04.250
SPEAKER 2: Thank
you, Mr. Bowman.

00:35:04.250 --> 00:35:07.173
We now have time for some
Q&amp;A. If you have a question,

00:35:07.173 --> 00:35:08.840
please make your way
to the microphones.

00:35:30.810 --> 00:35:31.950
SPEAKER 3: Thank you.

00:35:31.950 --> 00:35:33.100
Thank you very much.

00:35:33.100 --> 00:35:35.550
That was really
a wonderful talk.

00:35:35.550 --> 00:35:40.255
I just was really intrigued by
what you mentioned at the end.

00:35:40.255 --> 00:35:41.380
I'm sorry-- is this better.

00:35:41.380 --> 00:35:43.005
JAMES BOWMAN: That's
better, thank you.

00:35:43.005 --> 00:35:44.100
SPEAKER 3: Thank you.

00:35:44.100 --> 00:35:47.190
I have not seen all
of the new Emma movie,

00:35:47.190 --> 00:35:51.930
but I was not a fan of
it, just because it seemed

00:35:51.930 --> 00:35:54.000
to be making fun of the book.

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:55.680
But I was intrigued
by what you said

00:35:55.680 --> 00:35:58.320
that maybe it was
all just following

00:35:58.320 --> 00:35:59.910
the wrongness ideology.

00:35:59.910 --> 00:36:02.190
JAMES BOWMAN: It was all what?

00:36:02.190 --> 00:36:05.800
SPEAKER 3: That maybe that was
just the wrongness ideology,

00:36:05.800 --> 00:36:06.810
maybe?

00:36:06.810 --> 00:36:08.590
JAMES BOWMAN: Wrongology.

00:36:08.590 --> 00:36:10.710
SPEAKER 3: The
wrongology, that maybe all

00:36:10.710 --> 00:36:14.580
of those other seemingly
errors were just

00:36:14.580 --> 00:36:16.230
part of that
wrongology, and so I

00:36:16.230 --> 00:36:18.840
was wondering if you could
expand a little bit on that.

00:36:18.840 --> 00:36:21.670
JAMES BOWMAN: Well, when
somebody does something

00:36:21.670 --> 00:36:26.052
so right, you kind of
look back and think

00:36:26.052 --> 00:36:28.010
of all the other things
that they've done wrong

00:36:28.010 --> 00:36:30.510
in a different light.

00:36:30.510 --> 00:36:32.940
It seems to me.

00:36:32.940 --> 00:36:38.890
I just-- I needed
to see it again,

00:36:38.890 --> 00:36:43.220
obviously, after that ending,
because I thought, well,

00:36:43.220 --> 00:36:44.825
if she does this
right, then maybe she

00:36:44.825 --> 00:36:46.450
was doing the other
things right that I

00:36:46.450 --> 00:36:48.460
thought she was doing wrong.

00:36:48.460 --> 00:36:51.940
Maybe she could only have
produced this brilliant effect

00:36:51.940 --> 00:36:54.172
at the end by doing all
the other things wrong

00:36:54.172 --> 00:36:55.380
that I thought she did wrong.

00:37:05.357 --> 00:37:05.940
SPEAKER 4: Hi.

00:37:05.940 --> 00:37:07.580
I really enjoyed that lecture.

00:37:07.580 --> 00:37:09.440
At the beginning
you said something

00:37:09.440 --> 00:37:14.060
along the lines of Jane Austen
taking the social classes

00:37:14.060 --> 00:37:16.105
for granted in
her stories, and I

00:37:16.105 --> 00:37:18.230
was wondering if you could
expand a little bit more

00:37:18.230 --> 00:37:20.450
on what you meant
by that statement.

00:37:24.320 --> 00:37:25.820
JAMES BOWMAN: Well,
they were a fact

00:37:25.820 --> 00:37:31.070
of life, the social gradations.

00:37:31.070 --> 00:37:36.920
There's-- in the 2007 version
of Pride and Prejudice,

00:37:36.920 --> 00:37:42.340
there is some attempt,
I think it's 2006.

00:37:42.340 --> 00:37:43.640
2007?

00:37:43.640 --> 00:37:46.730
Anyway, it's the one
with Keira Knightley

00:37:46.730 --> 00:37:53.030
and Donald Sutherland as
a really not very good

00:37:53.030 --> 00:37:54.650
Mr. Bennett.

00:37:54.650 --> 00:37:58.130
But there was a lot
of effort in that--

00:37:58.130 --> 00:38:01.310
I should have mentioned that--

00:38:01.310 --> 00:38:06.790
to cast or turn the
camera on the servants

00:38:06.790 --> 00:38:10.940
and the lower-class
people who are taken

00:38:10.940 --> 00:38:15.590
for granted in most of the
most versions of Jane Austen,

00:38:15.590 --> 00:38:19.310
as a reminder that these
people live the way

00:38:19.310 --> 00:38:22.552
they live because of
the labors of people

00:38:22.552 --> 00:38:23.510
that they look down on.

00:38:26.330 --> 00:38:31.030
That's a very modern
way of looking at it.

00:38:31.030 --> 00:38:35.930
I think as best I can tell,
it's not Jane Austen's way.

00:38:35.930 --> 00:38:39.380
She too would take the
servants for granted.

00:38:39.380 --> 00:38:44.540
She too would recognize
that, as Mr. Elton says,

00:38:44.540 --> 00:38:46.760
everyone has their level.

00:38:46.760 --> 00:38:54.080
That it was a serious matter if
you married too far up or down

00:38:54.080 --> 00:38:56.690
from the class you
were born into,

00:38:56.690 --> 00:38:59.180
because it involved
your family, and that's

00:38:59.180 --> 00:39:06.550
the point Mr. Darcy was
making about Lizzie's family.

00:39:06.550 --> 00:39:09.420
It was a real consideration.

00:39:09.420 --> 00:39:13.400
And I think it's one that
Jane Austen would have shared,

00:39:13.400 --> 00:39:16.010
as she would most of
the things that seem

00:39:16.010 --> 00:39:21.320
to us in our egalitarian age
a much more worrisome thing.

00:39:21.320 --> 00:39:23.782
As I say, though, I think
we're kind of getting away

00:39:23.782 --> 00:39:24.740
from that a little bit.

00:39:24.740 --> 00:39:27.920
It's more of a
20th-century obsession,

00:39:27.920 --> 00:39:29.420
this idea with social classes.

00:39:29.420 --> 00:39:32.510
Now we have other classes
that we care more about.

00:39:32.510 --> 00:39:36.260
Race, sex, sexual orientation,
all that sort of thing

00:39:36.260 --> 00:39:40.170
is more important to us
than social hierarchies.

00:39:46.060 --> 00:39:47.390
SPEAKER 5: Hi.

00:39:47.390 --> 00:39:50.690
So at the very beginning
you talked about Austen

00:39:50.690 --> 00:39:54.200
loving theater, but kind
of hating those amateur

00:39:54.200 --> 00:39:57.080
plays that can be
just kind of base,

00:39:57.080 --> 00:39:59.377
and so I was just wondering
if you could speak

00:39:59.377 --> 00:40:01.460
a little bit about how she
writes these books that

00:40:01.460 --> 00:40:05.720
are these moral books,
but then they always

00:40:05.720 --> 00:40:08.480
have this kind of romantic,
or at least who's going

00:40:08.480 --> 00:40:11.220
to marry who aspect to them.

00:40:11.220 --> 00:40:15.290
And so kind of why that is a big
part of it when she's writing

00:40:15.290 --> 00:40:17.525
such books about morality?

00:40:17.525 --> 00:40:19.400
JAMES BOWMAN: When she's
writing books about?

00:40:19.400 --> 00:40:21.290
SPEAKER 5: Morality.

00:40:21.290 --> 00:40:25.550
JAMES BOWMAN: Well,
for a woman, that was

00:40:25.550 --> 00:40:28.850
the supreme area of morality.

00:40:28.850 --> 00:40:31.580
That was the morality
that mattered for women

00:40:31.580 --> 00:40:32.580
more than anything else.

00:40:32.580 --> 00:40:35.720
Because marriage mattered for
them more than anything else.

00:40:35.720 --> 00:40:38.960
It was the only way
they could escape from--

00:40:38.960 --> 00:40:42.560
I mean, women of the class that
Jane Austen is concerned with,

00:40:42.560 --> 00:40:46.520
the only way they could hope
to leave their father's house

00:40:46.520 --> 00:40:48.895
and move to a husband's house.

00:40:48.895 --> 00:40:51.020
You couldn't just go out
on your own in those days,

00:40:51.020 --> 00:40:55.130
and so marriage and
all that surrounds it,

00:40:55.130 --> 00:41:01.880
including the emotions
of love, which

00:41:01.880 --> 00:41:04.310
even then was pretty
important to people

00:41:04.310 --> 00:41:10.250
in looking for
marital partners, this

00:41:10.250 --> 00:41:13.310
became the central
concern of their lives.

00:41:13.310 --> 00:41:15.920
That's what Mrs.
Bennett is all about.

00:41:15.920 --> 00:41:19.430
I mean, she's a silly
woman, but she's only

00:41:19.430 --> 00:41:22.760
doing what any woman
in her situation

00:41:22.760 --> 00:41:26.587
would feel necessary
to do, which

00:41:26.587 --> 00:41:28.670
is to get her daughters
married however she could,

00:41:28.670 --> 00:41:33.870
because that's the only
way they could ever

00:41:33.870 --> 00:41:38.120
be independent of her.

00:41:38.120 --> 00:41:42.890
So morality for
a woman basically

00:41:42.890 --> 00:41:49.480
concerned sex and marriage,
and that was pretty much it.

00:41:49.480 --> 00:41:50.110
Yes?

00:41:50.110 --> 00:41:53.290
SPEAKER 6: So you mentioned that
Mr. Darcy had a very sincere

00:41:53.290 --> 00:41:54.370
confession of error.

00:41:54.370 --> 00:41:56.050
Did you find any
other characters

00:41:56.050 --> 00:41:59.630
in the other movies that had
similar sincerity moments?

00:41:59.630 --> 00:42:01.630
JAMES BOWMAN: I'm sorry,
I missed the beginning.

00:42:01.630 --> 00:42:03.713
SPEAKER 6: Did you think
that any other characters

00:42:03.713 --> 00:42:06.400
in the Jane Austen
movies had moments

00:42:06.400 --> 00:42:10.913
of sincerity similar to Mr.
Darcy's confession of error?

00:42:10.913 --> 00:42:11.830
JAMES BOWMAN: Oh, yes.

00:42:11.830 --> 00:42:14.380
Yes I think they all--

00:42:14.380 --> 00:42:19.030
well, all the good ones.

00:42:19.030 --> 00:42:22.240
They were all sincere, I think,
in confessing they're right.

00:42:22.240 --> 00:42:28.870
I think there must be some doubt
about Frank Churchill being

00:42:28.870 --> 00:42:31.757
totally sincere, anyway.

00:42:31.757 --> 00:42:34.090
Claiming to have known all
along that he was doing wrong

00:42:34.090 --> 00:42:40.210
but thinking that now that
he's got what he wanted to

00:42:40.210 --> 00:42:44.890
by doing it he can apologize
and everything will be OK.

00:42:44.890 --> 00:42:52.210
But I think in all the
other cases the sincerity is

00:42:52.210 --> 00:42:54.850
pretty obvious.

00:42:54.850 --> 00:43:00.890
There are other
occasions when people

00:43:00.890 --> 00:43:03.910
confess to being wrong
when it isn't sincere.

00:43:03.910 --> 00:43:06.560
I think Henry Crawford
is one example of that

00:43:06.560 --> 00:43:14.960
in Mansfield Park where he says,
I'm wrong, I know I'm wrong,

00:43:14.960 --> 00:43:19.760
tell me what's right to Fanny.

00:43:22.490 --> 00:43:27.350
Which is just another way of
his trying to get around her,

00:43:27.350 --> 00:43:29.180
get around her defenses.

00:43:29.180 --> 00:43:32.960
Well, this is what she
wants, that's what I'll do.

00:43:32.960 --> 00:43:36.780
But mostly the I
was wrong moment is

00:43:36.780 --> 00:43:38.960
a moment of sincerity, I think.

00:43:43.350 --> 00:43:45.108
Yes?

00:43:45.108 --> 00:43:45.900
SPEAKER 7: OK, sir.

00:43:45.900 --> 00:43:50.910
I thought a point you made
was very interesting about how

00:43:50.910 --> 00:43:54.540
this element in Jane Austen's
novels about the characters

00:43:54.540 --> 00:43:57.540
being wrong, and
realizing their wrongness

00:43:57.540 --> 00:44:00.960
is one of the elements that
makes them good stories.

00:44:00.960 --> 00:44:04.110
And then you brought up
some other film adaptions

00:44:04.110 --> 00:44:06.130
that did not address that.

00:44:06.130 --> 00:44:09.510
And he kind of skirted over
the issue of the characters

00:44:09.510 --> 00:44:13.320
being wrong and them realizing.

00:44:13.320 --> 00:44:15.630
And these were the
worst adaptions,

00:44:15.630 --> 00:44:21.800
so do you think that
in this culture,

00:44:21.800 --> 00:44:26.150
they purposely did not make the
characters address their wrong?

00:44:26.150 --> 00:44:28.490
Like, do you think
that's some fear

00:44:28.490 --> 00:44:32.480
on the part of the filmmakers
of admitting the fallibility

00:44:32.480 --> 00:44:33.470
of their characters?

00:44:33.470 --> 00:44:35.420
Or just what are your
thoughts on that?

00:44:35.420 --> 00:44:37.460
JAMES BOWMAN: No, I
think that they just

00:44:37.460 --> 00:44:40.700
didn't take it that seriously.

00:44:40.700 --> 00:44:48.510
If you compare the movie that
was shown this afternoon,

00:44:48.510 --> 00:44:55.070
Greer Garson's confession of
error seems almost gratuitous.

00:44:55.070 --> 00:44:57.650
I mean partly that's because
of the constraints of the film,

00:44:57.650 --> 00:45:01.100
but one minute she's saying I
hate you, the next minute she's

00:45:01.100 --> 00:45:03.470
saying I love him.

00:45:03.470 --> 00:45:04.430
What happened?

00:45:04.430 --> 00:45:09.200
It can't all be down
to just that in getting

00:45:09.200 --> 00:45:10.590
put right about Wickham.

00:45:13.430 --> 00:45:17.780
But it's just--
it's passed over,

00:45:17.780 --> 00:45:22.220
I think partly because
mainly because it

00:45:22.220 --> 00:45:24.270
was conceived of as a comedy.

00:45:24.270 --> 00:45:27.417
It was-- don't want
the laughing to stop.

00:45:27.417 --> 00:45:29.000
We don't want to get
too serious here.

00:45:29.000 --> 00:45:31.850
We don't want to say,
oh, my god, I was wrong.

00:45:31.850 --> 00:45:34.910
We were just, oh,
that was wrong,

00:45:34.910 --> 00:45:38.960
and then we move on to
the next funny episode.

00:45:38.960 --> 00:45:46.070
I think it's a way of looking
at a different-- looking

00:45:46.070 --> 00:45:49.280
at it dramatically from a
different point of view,

00:45:49.280 --> 00:45:53.540
as they did back in 1940,
and as I think now we're

00:45:53.540 --> 00:45:59.420
more inclined to do, is it's
a more serious business when

00:45:59.420 --> 00:46:03.965
we're wrong, and I think
we can understand it more.

00:46:03.965 --> 00:46:05.840
Maybe they were just
more used to being wrong

00:46:05.840 --> 00:46:06.632
back in those days.

00:46:09.620 --> 00:46:10.810
SPEAKER 8: Hi there.

00:46:10.810 --> 00:46:11.810
Thanks for your lecture.

00:46:11.810 --> 00:46:13.460
It was really, really good.

00:46:13.460 --> 00:46:16.800
The 1995 Pride and Prejudice
is objectively the best.

00:46:16.800 --> 00:46:17.978
So.

00:46:17.978 --> 00:46:18.770
JAMES BOWMAN: What?

00:46:18.770 --> 00:46:20.780
SPEAKER 8: The 1995
Pride and Prejudice

00:46:20.780 --> 00:46:22.310
is objectively the best.

00:46:22.310 --> 00:46:23.570
JAMES BOWMAN: Yes, yes.

00:46:23.570 --> 00:46:24.950
I agree.

00:46:24.950 --> 00:46:26.510
SPEAKER 8: So I
really like what you

00:46:26.510 --> 00:46:31.430
were saying about the kind of
tension between self-knowledge

00:46:31.430 --> 00:46:33.640
and what was the other?

00:46:33.640 --> 00:46:36.100
It was self-command.

00:46:36.100 --> 00:46:37.157
I think.

00:46:37.157 --> 00:46:38.490
JAMES BOWMAN: Self-command, yes.

00:46:38.490 --> 00:46:40.198
SPEAKER 8: Yeah, and
I was just wondering

00:46:40.198 --> 00:46:47.860
what insights you think Jane
Austen had as to those two

00:46:47.860 --> 00:46:51.220
virtues, I suppose.

00:46:51.220 --> 00:46:53.320
Did she have a
sense of which one

00:46:53.320 --> 00:46:58.980
was more commonly difficult
for a man to achieve,

00:46:58.980 --> 00:47:01.290
or which one was more difficult
for a woman to achieve,

00:47:01.290 --> 00:47:03.390
or is it just kind of
case-by-case basis?

00:47:07.170 --> 00:47:09.990
Are these virtues sometimes
more masculine and easier

00:47:09.990 --> 00:47:11.770
for men to achieve?

00:47:11.770 --> 00:47:15.330
I mean, I think that
Darcy seems to have

00:47:15.330 --> 00:47:19.410
some appearance of self-command,
but no self-knowledge.

00:47:19.410 --> 00:47:21.330
But then you could even
argue that he doesn't

00:47:21.330 --> 00:47:23.160
have self-command
because he can't really

00:47:23.160 --> 00:47:25.600
show who he really is.

00:47:25.600 --> 00:47:28.560
JAMES BOWMAN: No, that
showing who you really are

00:47:28.560 --> 00:47:31.630
is not a good thing
in Jane Austen.

00:47:31.630 --> 00:47:37.680
But I think you're right to
say that it's easier to assume

00:47:37.680 --> 00:47:41.040
it of a man than of a woman.

00:47:41.040 --> 00:47:44.130
For Jane Austen, I hesitate.

00:47:44.130 --> 00:47:52.200
Or, I hasten to add
that partly because it's

00:47:52.200 --> 00:47:53.280
an ideological thing.

00:47:53.280 --> 00:47:57.990
This is the point of
Sense and Sensibility,

00:47:57.990 --> 00:48:00.450
that Marianne is a
spokesman for a--

00:48:00.450 --> 00:48:07.720
or a spokeswoman for a-- new
way of looking at emotion.

00:48:07.720 --> 00:48:09.570
It was called
sensibility and it was

00:48:09.570 --> 00:48:12.180
a movement of the
Romantic period,

00:48:12.180 --> 00:48:14.460
and she's a very
romantic woman who

00:48:14.460 --> 00:48:18.200
thought that people ought
to show their feelings.

00:48:18.200 --> 00:48:20.400
It's a bad thing
to repress them.

00:48:20.400 --> 00:48:24.120
It's the beginnings
of our more modern way

00:48:24.120 --> 00:48:25.650
of looking at things.

00:48:25.650 --> 00:48:29.220
And Jane Austen is standing
pretty firmly against that,

00:48:29.220 --> 00:48:31.207
and saying this is what happens.

00:48:31.207 --> 00:48:32.790
This is what happens
when you do that,

00:48:32.790 --> 00:48:37.830
when you let your let your
feelings show who you really

00:48:37.830 --> 00:48:39.223
are.

00:48:39.223 --> 00:48:40.140
Don't want to do that.

00:48:40.140 --> 00:48:40.710
No.

00:48:40.710 --> 00:48:43.265
Particularly if you're a woman.

00:48:43.265 --> 00:48:44.640
It leads to all
kinds of trouble.

00:48:49.400 --> 00:48:50.210
Yes.

00:48:50.210 --> 00:48:50.960
SPEAKER 9: Oh, OK.

00:48:50.960 --> 00:48:55.490
So I was wondering, what are
your thoughts on that idea

00:48:55.490 --> 00:48:58.910
of wrongness in Lady Susan?

00:48:58.910 --> 00:49:03.710
I mean, because that's one
of the only novels that

00:49:03.710 --> 00:49:09.548
has someone who is that wrong.

00:49:09.548 --> 00:49:10.340
JAMES BOWMAN: Yeah.

00:49:10.340 --> 00:49:12.320
Yeah, you're right.

00:49:12.320 --> 00:49:16.530
And that's sort of why I
didn't mention Lady Susan.

00:49:16.530 --> 00:49:19.740
Because it completely
overthrows everything I said.

00:49:19.740 --> 00:49:20.240
No.

00:49:23.360 --> 00:49:30.080
No, it's-- I see Lady
Susan as apprentice work.

00:49:30.080 --> 00:49:34.280
Basically, she's learning her
trade when she wrote that.

00:49:34.280 --> 00:49:39.350
And I think she frankly
delighted in someone as wicked

00:49:39.350 --> 00:49:45.750
as Lady Susan Vernon, and she
just enjoyed that creation.

00:49:45.750 --> 00:49:51.660
She wasn't really in her
moralistic vein at that stage.

00:49:51.660 --> 00:49:55.550
She was just playing around
with the idea of someone who

00:49:55.550 --> 00:50:00.020
could be that calculating
and that wicked,

00:50:00.020 --> 00:50:02.390
and showing it in sort
of the indirect way

00:50:02.390 --> 00:50:06.020
through the epistolary style.

00:50:06.020 --> 00:50:08.090
It is-- but you're
right, it is--

00:50:08.090 --> 00:50:12.418
it's certainly unusual
in the whole corpus.

00:50:12.418 --> 00:50:14.210
She never wrote like
that [? away ?] again.

00:50:20.990 --> 00:50:23.420
SPEAKER 2: OK, this
is our last question.

00:50:23.420 --> 00:50:25.400
JAMES BOWMAN: Sure.

00:50:25.400 --> 00:50:26.150
SPEAKER 10: Hello.

00:50:26.150 --> 00:50:27.930
Thank you, that was great.

00:50:27.930 --> 00:50:29.510
Thank you, Mr. Bowman.

00:50:29.510 --> 00:50:33.170
You spoke of Jane Austen as
a moralist and the moralist

00:50:33.170 --> 00:50:35.190
we need.

00:50:35.190 --> 00:50:37.460
But there's moralism and
there's moralism, right?

00:50:37.460 --> 00:50:42.750
And with Jane
Austen, I just mean

00:50:42.750 --> 00:50:44.630
there's certain
kinds of moralism

00:50:44.630 --> 00:50:47.030
that you don't want
to sit through.

00:50:47.030 --> 00:50:54.170
But you don't really feel Jane
Austen's moralism as a burden,

00:50:54.170 --> 00:50:54.830
I guess?

00:50:54.830 --> 00:50:55.747
JAMES BOWMAN: As what?

00:50:55.747 --> 00:50:57.060
SPEAKER 10: A burden.

00:50:57.060 --> 00:50:57.590
It's--

00:50:57.590 --> 00:50:58.310
JAMES BOWMAN: A burden?

00:50:58.310 --> 00:50:58.977
SPEAKER 10: Yes.

00:50:58.977 --> 00:51:00.960
JAMES BOWMAN: You
don't feel it, though.

00:51:00.960 --> 00:51:04.700
SPEAKER 10: What I'm
saying is, why do we

00:51:04.700 --> 00:51:07.250
like Jane Austen's
moralism and not feel it

00:51:07.250 --> 00:51:10.760
as sermonic or something?

00:51:10.760 --> 00:51:15.248
Whereas there are many other
kinds of films and novels

00:51:15.248 --> 00:51:16.790
that we don't have
patience with that

00:51:16.790 --> 00:51:18.383
are trying to do
something similar.

00:51:18.383 --> 00:51:19.925
JAMES BOWMAN: That's
a good question.

00:51:22.680 --> 00:51:29.090
Well, I think it's because
she's so obviously sincere.

00:51:29.090 --> 00:51:33.740
She's so obviously engaged
with these very serious

00:51:33.740 --> 00:51:35.540
moral issues, and
not just preaching.

00:51:35.540 --> 00:51:37.430
She's showing them in action.

00:51:37.430 --> 00:51:40.220
She's showing how
people actually

00:51:40.220 --> 00:51:45.050
make decisions, good and bad,
and how they get things wrong.

00:51:45.050 --> 00:51:48.050
And then showing it in a
way that that's sympathetic.

00:51:48.050 --> 00:51:50.300
She understands how
their minds are working.

00:51:50.300 --> 00:51:52.190
She understands what's going on.

00:51:52.190 --> 00:51:56.870
She's not laying down
the law and saying, oh,

00:51:56.870 --> 00:51:57.920
isn't this terrible.

00:51:57.920 --> 00:51:59.390
She's just showing us.

00:51:59.390 --> 00:52:01.370
She just showing how it works.

00:52:01.370 --> 00:52:04.580
And we recognize
that because we've

00:52:04.580 --> 00:52:08.420
had similar experiences
ourselves, most of it.

00:52:08.420 --> 00:52:14.500
And so it's not
preachy, as you say.

00:52:14.500 --> 00:52:18.020
There's a great line
in Pride and Prejudice

00:52:18.020 --> 00:52:21.140
about Mary, the
serious daughter who's

00:52:21.140 --> 00:52:23.990
engaged in philosophical
studies and all that,

00:52:23.990 --> 00:52:27.140
about her threadbare moralism.

00:52:27.140 --> 00:52:29.390
She's the one that
always has a platitude

00:52:29.390 --> 00:52:33.170
ready for every occasion,
about ah, this is wrong,

00:52:33.170 --> 00:52:35.360
and this is right.

00:52:35.360 --> 00:52:38.990
But nobody pays any attention
to her because it's just words.

00:52:38.990 --> 00:52:42.440
It's just preaching.

00:52:42.440 --> 00:52:44.660
Whereas what Jane
Austen is doing

00:52:44.660 --> 00:52:47.120
is showing us the
thing in action.

00:52:47.120 --> 00:52:50.950
And that we can all
relate to, I think.

00:52:50.950 --> 00:52:52.220
SPEAKER 2: Thank you.

00:52:52.220 --> 00:52:55.270
[APPLAUSE]

